<div class="space-y-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight lg:text-5xl mb-4">
            <span class="text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-amber-400 animate-gradient-x">
                Authentication
            </span>
            <span class="text-white">with JWT and Passkeys</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            Implement secure, stateless authentication for your APIs and web applications using JSON Web Tokens, a second factor with <a href="#two-factor" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">authenticator apps</a>, and phishing-resistant sign-in with <a href="#passkeys" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">passkeys</a>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Overview Section -->
    <section>
        <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-900/20 to-orange-900/20 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-white/10 shadow-xl backdrop-blur-sm">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                <div class="p-3 bg-amber-500/20 rounded-lg text-amber-400">
                    <svg class="w-8 h-8" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-white mb-3 mt-0 border-none">Secure by Default</h2>
                    <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-0">
                        SoliLang includes first-class support for JWT (JSON Web Token). Unlike session-based auth, JWTs are stateless, making them perfect for microservices, mobile apps, and modern Single Page Applications (SPAs).
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Creating Tokens -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">1</span>
            Creating Tokens
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Use the <code class="text-amber-300">jwt_sign</code> function to create a signed token. You can include any custom claims in the payload.
        </p>
        
        <!-- Terminal Window -->
        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#1e1e1e] border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-red-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-yellow-500/80"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-green-500/80"></div>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono">controllers/auth_controller.sl</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># 1. Define your payload
payload = {
  "sub": "user_12345",
  "name": "Alice Wonderland",
  "role": "admin",
  "iat": now
}

# 2. Get your secret key securely
secret = getenv("JWT_SECRET")
# 3. Sign the token (default algorithm: HS256)
token = jwt_sign(payload, secret)
# Option: Set expiration (e.g., 1 hour from now)
token_with_exp = jwt_sign(
  payload, 
  secret, 
  { "expires_in": 3600 }
);</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Verifying Tokens -->
    <section>
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-orange-500/20 text-orange-400 text-sm border border-orange-500/30">2</span>
            Verifying Tokens
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
            Verify incoming tokens using <code class="text-orange-300">jwt_verify</code>. This function checks the signature and expiration automatically. Its options (<code>audience</code>, <code>issuer</code>, <code>subject</code>, <code>authorized_party</code>, <code>leeway</code>, <code>algorithm</code>, <code>key</code>) are listed under <a href="/docs/builtins/jwt#def-jwt-verify" class="text-orange-400 hover:text-orange-300">jwt_verify</a>; any other name (<code>aud</code> for <code>audience</code>, say) raises instead of being skipped.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden bg-[#1e1e1e] border border-white/10 shadow-2xl">
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-3 bg-white/5 border-b border-white/5">
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></div>
                <div class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-gray-600"></div>
                <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-gray-500 font-mono">middleware/auth.sl</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-6 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">result = jwt_verify(token, secret)
if result["error"] == true
  # Handle invalid token
  print("Auth Error:", result["message"])
else
  # Token is valid, access claims
  user_id = result["sub"]
  print("Authenticated User:", user_id)
end</code></pre>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Security Warning -->
    <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-yellow-400 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
            <svg class="w-5 h-5" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z" />
            </svg>
            Security Warning
        </h4>
        <p class="text-yellow-200/80 text-sm">
            Never hardcode your <code>JWT_SECRET</code> in your source code. Always use environment variables (<code>.env</code>)
or a secure secrets manager. If your secret is compromised, all issued tokens become insecure.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Two-factor -->
    <section id="two-factor" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-sm border border-emerald-500/30">3</span>
            Two-factor
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A second factor for password sign-in: the six-digit code of an authenticator app (TOTP, RFC 6238 &mdash;
            1Password, Google Authenticator, Authy&hellip;), with backup codes for a lost device.
        </p>

        <h3 id="two-factor-generate" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Generate it</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth --two-factor</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">
            On a new app this writes session auth and two-factor together; on an app that already ran
            <code class="text-amber-300">soli generate auth</code> it adds only the two-factor files. It combines with
            <code class="text-amber-300">--passkeys</code>. <strong class="text-white">It edits no existing file</strong>: everything
            below is new, and nothing is overwritten.
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">File</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">What it does</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/models/two_factor.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>TwoFactor</code>, one record per user: the secret (encrypted), the last accepted time step, the backup-code digests, the wrong-code counter</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/middleware/two_factor.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The gate: a session that passed one factor only is a guest, and its page loads go to the challenge</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/controllers/two_factor_controller.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>GET /two_factor</code>, <code>POST /two_factor/setup</code>, <code>POST /two_factor/confirm</code>, <code>POST /two_factor/backup_codes</code>, <code>DELETE /two_factor</code>, <code>GET</code> and <code>POST /two_factor/challenge</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/views/two_factor/*.html.slv</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Settings, enrolment (QR code and key), backup codes, challenge</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>db/migrations/…_create_two_factors.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Unique index on <code>user_id</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Run <code>soli db:migrate up</code>, set <code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_ENCRYPTION_KEY</code> (the secret is stored with
            <a href="/docs/database/models#encrypted-attributes" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">encrypts</a>;
            <code>Crypto.random_hex(32)</code> makes one), and link to <code>/two_factor</code> from your account page.
            <code class="text-amber-300">TWO_FACTOR_ISSUER</code> is the name authenticator apps show next to the account.
        </p>

        <h3 id="two-factor-how" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">How it works</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Enrolling.</strong> <code>/two_factor</code> &rarr; <em>Set up</em> shows the secret as a QR code (<code class="text-amber-300">qr_svg</code>, rendered on the server) and as text. Two-factor turns on only once the user typed back a first code, and the page then shows ten backup codes, once. Turning it on signs every other session of the account out.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Signing in.</strong> The password step is unchanged. The middleware runs after <code>load_current_user</code>: if the user has two-factor on and this session has not passed the challenge, the request continues as a guest (<code>current_user()</code> is <code>nil</code>), and a <code>GET</code> is redirected to <code>/two_factor/challenge</code>. Because the gate is on the session rather than in the sign-in action, it covers every way a session opens &mdash; password, password reset, the remember-me cookie, OAuth &mdash; and a path you add later.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Codes.</strong> A code is accepted once: the record keeps the time step of the last accepted code and refuses that step and earlier ones (<a href="/docs/builtins/crypto#def-totp-step" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">Crypto.totp_step</a>). A backup code works once, typed with or without its dash. Codes may be typed with spaces.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Wrong codes.</strong> The endpoints share the sign-in form&rsquo;s per-IP throttle. <code class="text-amber-300">AUTH_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS</code> wrong codes in a row lock the second factor for <code class="text-amber-300">AUTH_LOCKOUT_SECONDS</code>; while locked, a right code is answered like a wrong one. The counter lives on the <code>TwoFactor</code> record, not the user: the password step clears the user&rsquo;s own counter, so anyone holding the password could otherwise reset it between guesses.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Turning it off, new backup codes.</strong> Both ask for the password (an account created through OAuth has none, and is not asked); turning it off also asks for a code.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">The owner, at the keyboard.</strong> Setting it up needs a sign-in from the last 15 minutes (<code>AUTH_FRESH_SECONDS</code>), and none of setup, backup codes or turning it off answers to an API token or to an administrator acting as the user (<code>403</code>). Backup codes are 80 random bits each (<code>xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx-xxxxx</code>), and wrong codes are counted in the database, one atomic write each. The model declares <code>optimistic_lock</code>: recording a code as used is a compare-and-swap, so two requests carrying the same code cannot both pass.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="two-factor-others" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">With remember-me, passkeys and OAuth</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Sign-in</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Second factor</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Password</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Asked</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Password reset link</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Asked &mdash; a mailbox alone is one factor</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Remember-me cookie</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Asked when the session it restores is new: the cookie replaces the password, not the code</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">OAuth (<code>soli generate oauth</code>)</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Asked</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Passkey (<code>--passkeys</code>)</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Skipped when the passkey verified its user (screen lock, PIN, biometrics): that is already two factors</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A passkey scaffold generated before <code class="text-amber-300">--two-factor</code> existed does not mark its session, so its
            sign-in is followed by the challenge; the generator prints the one line to add to
            <code>passkey_sessions#create</code>:
        </p>
        <pre data-filename="app/controllers/passkey_sessions_controller.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm">session_set("two_factor", "ok:#{user._key}") if result["user_verified"]</code></pre>
        <div class="callout callout-warning mt-4">
            <p class="text-yellow-200/80 text-sm mb-0">
                <strong>Limits.</strong> Authenticator apps only: no SMS or email codes, and no &ldquo;trust this device for 30 days&rdquo;.
                There is no admin screen to reset a user who lost both device and backup codes &mdash; delete their
                <code>TwoFactor</code> record. A session open on another device when a user turns two-factor <em>off</em>
                keeps its state until it ends.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section id="magic-link" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-sm border border-emerald-500/30">4</span>
            Magic link
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Sign in with a link sent by email, no password typed.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth --magic-link
soli db:migrate up</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">It adds <code class="text-amber-300">MagicLink</code> (model), <code class="text-amber-300">MagicLinksController</code> (<code>GET</code> and <code>POST /login/link</code>, <code>GET /login/link/:token</code>), <code class="text-amber-300">MagicLinkMailer</code> and its view, and edits nothing: link to <code>/login/link</code> from your sign-in view.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><strong class="text-white">One use, fifteen minutes</strong> (<code>MAGIC_LINK_TTL</code>), and only the newest link of an account works.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Only in the browser that asked.</strong> Asking sets a cookie; the link needs it. A link opened by a mail scanner, forwarded, or opened on another device signs nobody in &mdash; and is not used up by the attempt.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">No account probing.</strong> The answer, cookie included, is the same whether or not the address has an account. A locked account gets no link, nor does a single-sign-on-only one (<a href="/docs/security/saml" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">SAML</a>, <code>user.sso_only?</code>), and a link issued to one before is refused like an expired one.</li>
            <li>It shares the sign-in form&rsquo;s per-IP throttle, and only digests are stored.</li>
            <li>Opening the link confirms the address. With <a href="#two-factor" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">two-factor</a> on, the code challenge follows.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="api-tokens" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-sm border border-emerald-500/30">5</span>
            API tokens
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Personal access tokens: a user&rsquo;s credential for scripts and other services.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth --api-tokens
soli db:migrate up

curl -H "Authorization: Bearer soli_pat_…" https://app.example.com/invoices</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">It adds <code class="text-amber-300">ApiToken</code> (model), a middleware that authenticates the header (<code># order: 7</code>), and <code class="text-amber-300">ApiTokensController</code> with its page at <code>/api_tokens</code>: create (name, scopes, lifetime), list, revoke.</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Shown once.</strong> Only the token&rsquo;s SHA-256 digest is stored; the list shows its first characters, its scopes, its expiry and when it was last used (noted at most once a minute).</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">A request with a token runs as its user</strong>: <code>current_user()</code> is them, and <code>req["api_token"]</code> is the token. An unknown or expired token is a <code>401</code>, never a fall back to the session.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Scopes are yours.</strong> <code class="text-amber-300">API_TOKEN_SCOPES</code> in <code>app/models/api_token.sl</code> lists them (<code>read</code>, <code>write</code> to start with); guard an action with <code class="text-amber-300">api_scope?</code>. A request signed in with the session is not limited by scopes.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Managing tokens takes a person in a browser</strong>: a request made with a token cannot list, create or revoke tokens.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">The account&rsquo;s own pages are closed to tokens</strong>, whatever their scopes: <code>/login</code>, <code>/password</code>, <code>/passkeys</code>, <code>/two_factor</code>, <code>/api_tokens</code>, <code>/impersonate</code>, <code>/oauth</code>, <code>/saml</code>, <code>/privacy</code> and the rest of <code>API_TOKEN_CLOSED_PATHS</code> (in the middleware) answer <code>403</code>. A token that reached them could register a passkey or an authenticator, or start an impersonation, and so become a browser session with all of its owner&rsquo;s rights. Add your own account pages to the list.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Creating one needs a recent sign-in</strong> (<code>AUTH_FRESH_SECONDS</code>, 15 minutes): not a session left open, nor one restored by the remember-me cookie.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">A password reset deletes them</strong>, with the account&rsquo;s passkeys and pending sign-in links: whoever had the account for a while keeps nothing they added. A password lockout does not stop a token &mdash; anyone who knows the address can trip it.</li>
            <li>A token is its own credential: the two-factor challenge does not apply to it. Give it the narrowest scopes and a lifetime.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">None for a single-sign-on-only account</strong> (<a href="/docs/security/saml" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">SAML</a>, <code>user.sso_only?</code>): creating one answers <code>403</code>, and a token made before is refused like an unknown one &mdash; it would outlive the user&rsquo;s removal at the identity provider.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre data-filename="app/controllers/invoices_controller.sl"><code class="language-soli text-sm"># POST /invoices
def create
  return render_json({"error": "scope"}, 403) unless api_scope?("write")
  # …
end</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section id="impersonation" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-sm border border-emerald-500/30">6</span>
            Impersonation
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">An administrator acts as a user, to see what they see.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth --impersonation</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">It adds <code class="text-amber-300">ImpersonationsController</code> (<code>POST /impersonate/:id</code>, <code>DELETE /impersonate</code>), two view helpers (<code>impersonating?()</code>, <code>impersonator()</code>) and a banner partial to render in your layout:</p>
        <pre data-filename="app/views/layouts/application.html.slv"><code class="language-erb text-sm">&lt;%- partial("impersonations/banner") %&gt;</code></pre>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mt-4">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Who may</strong>: an administrator, on an account that is not one, and not from inside an impersonation &mdash; <code>_may_impersonate</code> in the controller is the rule to change. Anything else is a <code>403</code>.</li>
            <li>While it lasts the session is the user&rsquo;s (<code>current_user()</code> is them) under a new session id, and the banner shows on every page with a button back.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Every start and stop is logged</strong>, and written to the <a href="/docs/database/models#audit-log" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">audit log</a> when the app has one (<code>action</code>: <code>impersonate</code> / <code>stop_impersonating</code>).</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">It ends with the administrator&rsquo;s own sessions, and after an hour.</strong> The session remembers the administrator&rsquo;s session version: a password reset, a demotion or a revocation of the administrator ends the impersonation on its next request, and stopping it then gives no administrator session back. <code>AUTH_IMPERSONATION_SECONDS</code> (3600) is its longest life.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">From a browser session only</strong>: an API token cannot start one.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Nothing that adds or removes a way in</strong> while impersonating: the user&rsquo;s API tokens, passkeys and two-factor settings answer <code>403</code> (<code>auth_own_session?(req)</code> &mdash; use it on sensitive pages of yours, a password or an email change).</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Passkeys -->
    <section id="passkeys" class="scroll-mt-20">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-sm border border-emerald-500/30">7</span>
            Passkeys
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Passkeys (WebAuthn) replace the password with a key pair the user's device keeps: the server stores only a
            public key, and a signature over a fresh challenge proves possession. They cannot be phished &mdash; the
            browser binds every response to the site's origin &mdash; and there is nothing to leak or reuse.
        </p>

        <h3 id="passkeys-generate" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Generate it</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth --passkeys</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">
            On a new app this writes session auth and passkeys together; on an app that already ran
            <code class="text-amber-300">soli generate auth</code> it adds only the passkey files. The flag goes before or
            after the folder, and no existing file is overwritten:
        </p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">File</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">What it does</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/models/passkey_credential.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>PasskeyCredential</code> (one row per passkey) and the challenge helpers</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/controllers/passkeys_controller.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>GET /passkeys</code>, <code>POST /passkeys/options</code>, <code>POST /passkeys</code>, <code>DELETE /passkeys/:id</code> &mdash; the signed-in user's passkeys</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/controllers/passkey_sessions_controller.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>POST /passkeys/login/options</code>, <code>POST /passkeys/login</code> &mdash; sign-in</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/views/passkeys/index.html.slv</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">List, add, remove</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/views/passkeys/_sign_in.html.slv</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The sign-in button, for <code>partial("passkeys/sign_in")</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>public/js/passkeys.js</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The browser side, no dependency</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>db/migrations/…_create_passkey_credentials.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Unique index on <code>credential_id</code>, index on <code>user_id</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            On a fresh app the sign-in view already renders the partial, and its email field has
            <code class="text-amber-300">autocomplete="username webauthn"</code> so the browser also offers passkeys in its
            autofill (conditional UI). An existing sign-in view is left alone: add
            <code class="text-amber-300">&lt;%- partial("passkeys/sign_in") %&gt;</code> and the <code>autocomplete</code>
            value yourself. Run <code>soli db:migrate up</code>, sign in, and add a passkey at <code>/passkeys</code>.
        </p>

        <h3 id="passkeys-config" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Relying party and origins</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Variable</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Default</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold"></th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">WEBAUTHN_RP_ID</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the host of <code>APP_BASE_URL</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The domain passkeys belong to; set the parent domain (<code>example.com</code>) to share them with <code>app.example.com</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">WEBAUTHN_ORIGINS</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>APP_BASE_URL</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Comma-separated, exact origins (scheme, host, port) a ceremony may come from</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-400">WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the RP ID</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Shown by the browser</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Browsers allow passkeys on <code>https://</code> and on <code>http://localhost</code> only. The default
            <code>APP_BASE_URL</code> (<code>http://localhost:5011</code>) works in development as long as you browse that
            exact origin; behind a proxy, set <code>APP_BASE_URL</code> to the public <code>https://</code> URL.
        </p>

        <h3 id="passkeys-sign-in" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">How sign-in works</h3>
        <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><code>POST /passkeys/login/options</code> returns request options with an empty <code>allowCredentials</code> (discoverable credentials: no username needed) and keeps the challenge in the session under <code>webauthn_challenge</code>, tagged with its purpose and an expiry (<code>WEBAUTHN_CHALLENGE_TTL</code>, 300&nbsp;s).</li>
            <li>The browser signs it; <code>passkeys.js</code> posts the credential's <code>toJSON()</code>.</li>
            <li><code>POST /passkeys/login</code> takes the challenge out of the session <em>before</em> verifying (a replay or a second tab fails closed), finds the passkey by <code>credential_id</code> with <code>find_by</code>, verifies it with <code>WebAuthn.verify_authentication</code>, and checks that the user handle is present and names the same account.</li>
            <li>It then opens the session exactly as the password login does: the per-IP throttle (<code>AUTH_ATTEMPTS_PER_IP</code>), the locked and confirmed gates, <code>session_regenerate()</code>, <code>user_id</code> and <code>session_version</code>.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A single-sign-on-only account (<a href="/docs/security/saml" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">SAML</a>, <code>user.sso_only?</code>) registers
            no passkey (<code>POST /passkeys/options</code> and <code>POST /passkeys</code> answer <code>403</code>), and one it
            registered before does not sign in.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Every failure &mdash; unknown credential, bad signature, expired challenge, locked account, a single-sign-on-only
            account, a sign counter that went backwards &mdash; gets the same <code>401</code>, so the endpoint reveals nothing about which passkeys exist.
            The JSON requests carry the page's CSRF token in <code>X-CSRF-Token</code> (the views emit it in
            <code>data-csrf-token</code>), so they also pass with <code>SOLI_CSRF_TOKENS=require</code>.
        </p>

        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">Adding a passkey needs the owner&rsquo;s own browser session and a sign-in from the last 15 minutes (<code>AUTH_FRESH_SECONDS</code>): an API token, an administrator acting as the user, or a session restored by the remember-me cookie gets <code>403</code>. A password reset deletes the account&rsquo;s passkeys. A password lockout does not stop a passkey sign-in.</p>
        <h3 id="passkeys-native" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Hand-rolled ceremonies</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The generated code is a thin layer over the native <code class="text-amber-300">WebAuthn</code> class &mdash;
            <code>registration_options</code>, <code>verify_registration</code>, <code>authentication_options</code>,
            <code>verify_authentication</code> &mdash; documented in
            <a href="/docs/builtins/webauthn" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">Built-ins &rarr; WebAuthn</a>.
            Use it directly for an API or a different storage model.
        </p>
        <div class="callout callout-warning">
            <p class="text-yellow-200/80 text-sm mb-0">
                <strong>Limits.</strong> Attestation is never trusted against a vendor root (the options ask for <code>"none"</code>), so passkeys
                cannot be restricted to certain authenticator models. ES256, EdDSA (Ed25519) and RS256 are supported; a packed self-attestation is checked, a certificate chain only reported.
                Passkeys are an additional credential: an account still has a password.
            </p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4"><strong class="text-white">In a native app.</strong> <code class="text-cyan-400">AppLinks.android(…, {"passkeys": true})</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">AppLinks.apple(…, {"passkeys": true})</code> let the app use the site&rsquo;s passkeys, and <code class="text-cyan-400">AppLinks.android_origin(fingerprint)</code> gives the <code class="text-cyan-400">android:apk-key-hash:…</code> origin to add to <code class="text-cyan-400">WEBAUTHN_ORIGINS</code> &mdash; see <a href="/docs/native/deep-links#passkeys" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Deep links → Passkeys in the app</a>.</p>
    </section>

</div>